research innovation Erasmus and the  migration crisis looks set to be the  European priorities for 2017 in any case  it certainly appeared that way in the  budget proposed by the member states  this summer now it's time for the MEP s  who share the power to adopt EE use  annual budget to debate their priorities  European budget is special in that it  contains two types of budget line on the  one hand there are commitments or legal  promises to spend money on certain  projects either in the same year as the  commitment or later and on the other  hand there are payments money that the  EU actually expects to pay out in a  given year the Member States draft  budget contains a seven percent decrease  in payments and a 0.9 percent increase  in commitments compared to the 2016 EU  budget on the side of the payments the  bulk of the budget is aimed at  supporting smart and inclusive growth  the big beneficiaries would be research  and innovation the European fund for  strategic investments and erasmus+ in  addition to this 5.2 billion euros in  commitments would be allocated for the  effective management of migration flows  and addressing root causes of migration  and finally security in the EU should be  hands through nearly 750 million euros  in payments this Wednesday MEP s will  debate the 2017 draft budget to define  their position for the following day  when the console Parliament and  Commission meet in trialogue over the  question  I
 

